{"questions":[{"question_id":"1l23g3vpe","paperTitle":"IIT-JEE 2012 Paper 2 Offline","year":2012,"examDate":2012,"subject":"chemistry","chapter":"p-block-elements","chapterGroup":"inorganic-chemistry","topic":"group-17-elements-halogen-family","type":"mcq","marks":3,"negMarks":1,"permalink":"p25-ml-of-household-bleach-solution-was-mixed-with-30-ml-o-jee-advanced-chemistry-some-basic-concepts-of-chemistry-ozwfiyjpsb8e0nsk","content":"<p>25 mL of household bleach solution was mixed with 30 mL of 0.50 M KI and 10 mL of 4 N acetic acid. In the titration of the liberated iodine, 48 mL of 0.25 N Na<sub>2</sub>S<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub> was used to reach the end point. The molarity of the household bleach solution is</p>","options":[{"identifier":"A","content":"0.48 M"},{"identifier":"B","content":"0.96 M"},{"identifier":"C","content":"0.24 M"},{"identifier":"D","content":"0.024 M"}],"correct_options":["C"],"explanation":"<p>Consider the titration reaction</p>\n<p>CaOCl<sub>2</sub> + 2KI <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.262ex\" height=\"1.181ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -511 1000 522\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"2192\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2192\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> I<sub>2</sub> + Ca(OH)<sub>2</sub> + KCl</p>\n<p>According to this reaction, 25 mL of CaOCl<sub>2</sub> reacts with 30 mL of 0.50 M KI</p>\n<p>I<sub>2</sub>  + 2Na<sub>2</sub>S<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub> <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.262ex\" height=\"1.181ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -511 1000 522\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"2192\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2192\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> Na<sub>2</sub>S<sub>4</sub>O<sub>6</sub> + 2NaI</p>\n<p>Given that 48 mL of 0.25 N Na<sub>2</sub>S<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub> was used to reach the end point. So, the number of moles of I<sub>2</sub> produced = 48 <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: 0.02ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.76ex\" height=\"1.09ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -491 778 482\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"D7\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-D7\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> 0.25/2 = 6.</p>\n<p>According to the reaction,</p>\n<p>Number of millimoles of bleaching powder = Number of moles of I<sub>2</sub> = (1/2) <mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: 0.02ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.76ex\" height=\"1.09ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -491 778 482\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mo\"><use data-c=\"D7\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-D7\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> Number of moles of Na<sub>2</sub>S<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub> = 6</p>\n<p>So, the molarity of CaOCl<sub>2</sub> is</p>\n<p><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -1.108ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"46.591ex\" height=\"3.334ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -983.5 20593.2 1473.